(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_gravitation=[{id:"neet-2026r-q015",pn:15,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Escape speed",q:"Two planets P\u2081 and P\u2082 with equal mass have radii R\u2081 and R\u2082, respectively, where R\u2082 = R\u2081/2. The escape speeds of P\u2081 and P\u2082 are v\u2081 and v\u2082, respectively. Then v\u2082/v\u2081 is:",o:["1","\u221A2","2","1/\u221A2"],c:1,s:`Escape speed v_e = \u221A(2gR) = \u221A(2GM/R).
M is the same for both, so v_e \u221D 1/\u221AR.
v\u2082/v\u2081 = \u221A(R\u2081/R\u2082) = \u221A2.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q026",pn:26,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Kepler's law of periods",q:"In a solar system, the time-period of revolution of a planet tracing a circular orbit of radius R is proportional to:",o:["R^(3/2)","R\xB2","R\xB3","R^(1/2)"],c:0,s:`In planetary motion, T\xB2 \u221D R\xB3.
So T \u221D R^(3/2).`},{id:"neet-2026-q027",pn:27,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Gravitational potential energy",q:"The amount of work done to raise a mass m from the surface of the Earth to a height equal to the radius of the Earth R will be",o:["2mgR","mgR","mgR/4","mgR/2"],c:3,s:`Work done = U\u2082 \u2212 U\u2081.
At height R the distance from the centre is 2R, so U\u2082 = \u2212GMm/(R + R) = \u2212GMm/(2R).
U\u2081 = \u2212GMm/R.
W = \u2212GMm/(2R) + GMm/R = GMm/(2R).
Using GM = gR\xB2, W = mgR/2.`},{id:"neet-2025-q031",pn:31,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Kepler's laws",q:"The radius of Martian orbit around the Sun is about 4 times the radius of the orbit of Mercury. The Martian year is 687 Earth days. Then which of the following is the length of 1 year on Mercury?",o:["88 earth days","225 earth days","172 earth days","124 earth days"],c:0,s:`Apply Kepler\u2019s 3rd law: T\xB2 \u221D R\xB3.
Radius of Martian orbit R\u2032 = 4R.
(T\u2032/T)\xB2 = (R\u2032/R)\xB3 = (4R/R)\xB3 = 4\xB3 = 64.
So T\u2032/T = 8.
Length of 1 year on Mercury T = T\u2032/8 = 687/8 = 85.88 days.
Note: printed answer (1) 88 days; the working gives 85.88 days, which is closest to 88 (Mercury's year is about 88 days)`},{id:"neet-2025-q032",pn:32,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Variation of g with height",q:"A body weighs 48 N on the surface of the earth. The gravitational force experienced by the body due to the earth at a height equal to one-third the radius of the earth from its surface is :",o:["16 N","27 N","32 N","36 N"],c:1,s:`W = mg, with g = GM/R\xB2 and g_h = GM/(R+h)\xB2.
W_h/W = mg_h/mg = g_h/g = R\xB2/(R+h)\xB2, with h = R/3.
W_h/W = R\xB2/(R + R/3)\xB2 = R\xB2/(4R/3)\xB2 = 9/16.
W_h = (9/16)W = (9/16) \xD7 48 (W = 48 N).
W_h = 27 N.`},{id:"neet-2024-q024",pn:24,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Acceleration due to gravity",q:"The mass of a planet is (1)/(10)th that of the earth and its diameter is half that of earth. The acceleration due to gravity on that planet is-",o:["4.9 m s\u207B\xB2","3.92 m s\u207B\xB2","19.6 m s\u207B\xB2","9.8 m s\u207B\xB2"],c:1,s:`g\u2032 = GM\u2032/R\u2032\xB2, with M\u2032 = M/10 and R\u2032 = R/2.
g\u2032 = (GM/10)/(R/2)\xB2 = (4/10)(GM/R\xB2).
g\u2032 = 0.4 \xD7 9.8 = 3.92 m s\u207B\xB2.`},{id:"neet-2024-q049",pn:49,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Satellite launch energy",q:"The minimum energy required to launch a satellite of mass m from the surface of earth of mass M and radius R in a circular orbit at an altitude of 2R from the surface of the earth is:",o:["GmM/(2R)","GmM/(3R)","5GmM/(6R)","2GmM/(3R)"],c:2,s:`Use energy conservation: U_i + K_i = U_f + K_f.
Initially U_i = \u2212GMm/R. At altitude 2R the orbit radius is 3R, so U_f = \u2212GMm/(3R).
Orbital speed at 3R satisfies v\xB2 = GM/(3R), so K_f = \xBD m \xD7 GM/(3R).
\u2212GMm/R + K_i = \u2212GMm/(3R) + \xBD \xD7 m \xD7 GM/(3R).
K_i = \u2212(1/6)(GMm/R) + GMm/R.
K_i = (5/6) GMm/R.`},{id:"neet-2023-q010",pn:10,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Gravitational potential and field",q:"Two bodies of mass m and 9 m are placed at a distance R. The gravitational potential on the line joining the bodies where the gravitational field equals zero, will be (G = gravitational constant):",o:["\u221220 Gm/R","\u22128 Gm/R","\u221212 Gm/R","\u221216 Gm/R"],c:3,s:`Let the zero-field point Q be at distance x from mass m, so R \u2212 x from mass 9m.
Fields cancel: Gm/x\xB2 = G(9m)/(R \u2212 x)\xB2.
So (R \u2212 x)\xB2/x\xB2 = 9, which gives x = R/4.
Potential at Q = \u2212Gm/x \u2212 G(9m)/(R \u2212 x).
= \u2212Gm/(R/4) \u2212 9Gm/(3R/4) = \u22124Gm/R \u2212 12Gm/R = \u221216Gm/R.`,sfig:{w:300,h:110,alt:"Line of length R: mass m at the left end, 9m at the right end, point Q at distance x from m and R \u2212 x from 9m.",d:[["l",150,22,30,22,"a"],["l",150,22,270,22,"a"],["t",150,16,"R","m"],["l",30,60,270,60],["c",30,60,3,"F"],["c",90,60,3,"F"],["c",270,60,3,"F"],["t",30,50,"m","m"],["t",270,50,"9m","m"],["t",90,88,"Q","m"],["l",60,76,32,76,"a"],["l",60,76,88,76,"a"],["t",60,72,"x","m"],["l",180,76,92,76,"a"],["l",180,76,268,76,"a"],["l",60,76,32,76,"a"],["l",60,76,88,76,"a"],["t",60,72,"x","m"],["l",180,76,92,76,"a"],["l",180,76,268,76,"a"],["t",180,72,"(R \u2212 x)","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2023-q045",pn:45,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Time period of near-earth satellite",q:"A satellite is orbiting just above the surface of the earth with period T. If d is the density of the earth and G is the universal constant of gravitation, the quantity 3\u03C0/(Gd) represents :",o:["\u221AT","T","T\xB2","T\xB3"],c:2,s:`For a satellite just above the surface, T = 2\u03C0\u221A(R\xB3/GM).
M = (4/3)\u03C0R\xB3d, so T = 2\u03C0\u221A(3/(4\u03C0Gd)).
Squaring, T\xB2 = 4\u03C0\xB2 \xD7 3/(4\u03C0Gd) = 3\u03C0/(Gd).
So the quantity is T\xB2.`},{id:"neet-2022-q003",pn:3,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Gravitational field intensity",q:"A body of mass 60 g experiences a gravitational force of 3.0 N, when placed at a particular point. The magnitude of the gravitational field intensity at that point is",o:["50 N/kg","20 N/kg","180 N/kg","0.05 N/kg"],c:0,s:`Gravitational field intensity is force per unit mass, F = mE.
Mass = 60 g = 60/1000 kg.
3 = (60/1000) \xD7 E, so E = 50 N/kg.`},{id:"neet-2022-q039",pn:39,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Dimensions of gravitational quantities",q:"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (a) Gravitational constant (G) (b) Gravitational potential energy (c) Gravitational potential (d) Gravitational intensity. List-II: (i) [L\xB2T\u207B\xB2] (ii) [M\u207B\xB9L\xB3T\u207B\xB2] (iii) [LT\u207B\xB2] (iv) [ML\xB2T\u207B\xB2]. Choose the correct answer from the options given below",o:["(a) - (ii), (b) - (iv), (c) - (i), (d) - (iii)","(a) - (ii), (b) - (iv), (c) - (iii), (d) - (i)","(a) - (iv), (b) - (ii), (c) - (i), (d) - (iii)","(a) - (ii), (b) - (i), (c) - (iv), (d) - (iii)"],c:0,s:`(a) G = Fr\xB2/(m\u2081m\u2082) = [MLT\u207B\xB2][L\xB2]/[M\xB2] = [M\u207B\xB9L\xB3T\u207B\xB2].
(b) Potential energy has dimensions [ML\xB2T\u207B\xB2].
(c) Potential = energy/mass = [L\xB2T\u207B\xB2].
(d) Intensity = force/mass = [LT\u207B\xB2].
So (a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii).`}]})();
